D|.\n\nSo, the sum is p + q + 195 + r + 367 + 562.\n\nWhich is p + q + r + 195 + 367 + 562.\n\nCompute 195 + 367 + 562: 195 + 367 is 562, plus 562 is 1124.\n\nSo, sum of pairwise intersections is p + q + r + 1124.\n\nSimilarly, sum of triple intersections is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|.\n\nWhich is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + p + q + r.\n\nSo, putting it back into the inclusion-exclusion equation:\n\n900 = 2024 - (p + q + r + 1124) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + p + q + r) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nSimplify step by step:\n\n900 = 2024 - p - q - r - 1124 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + p + q + r - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\n- The -p - q - r and +p + q + r cancel out.\n\nSo, 900 = 2024 - 1124 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nCompute 2024 - 1124: 2024 - 1124 is 900.\n\nSo, 900 = 900 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nWait, that simplifies to:\n\n900 = 900 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nSubtract 900 from both sides:\n\n0 = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nWhich implies |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nBut |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| is the number of people who own all four things, which is exactly what we need to find.\n\nSo, the equation is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = x, where x is the number of people who own all four.\n\nBut we need another equation to find x.\n\nWait, perhaps we can use the information about the number of people owning exactly two or three things.\n\nGiven that 437 residents own exactly two of these things, and 234 own exactly three.\n\nBut since everyone owns candy hearts, if someone owns exactly three things, they must own three of A, B, C, and D. But since D is everyone, so they must own exactly three of A, B, C, which implies they own all three, right? Because they have to own D as well.\n\nWait, that might not be the case. If someone owns exactly three 