n B and C and D\n- Similarly, let b = number of people who do not own B, but own A and C and D\n- Let c = number of people who do not own C, but own A and B and D\n\nThen, the number of people owning exactly three things is a + b + c = 234.\n\nSimilarly, the number of people owning exactly two things would be the number of people who do not own exactly two of A, B, C, but own D. Let me denote:\n\n- Let w = number of people who do not own A and do not own B, but own C and D\n- Let x = number of people who do not own A and do not own C, but own B and D\n- Let y = number of people who do not own B and do not own C, but own A and D\n\nThen, the number of people owning exactly two things is w + x + y = 437.\n\nNow, let's think about the total number of people.\n\nTotal residents: 900.\n\nThese 900 people are all in D, so we can think of them as being in the union of A, B, C, D. Since D is everyone, the union is everyone, so we can model this as the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nBut perhaps another approach is to think in terms of the Venn diagram. Since D is everyone, the Venn diagram would have four circles: A, B, C, and D, but D covers everyone.\n\nSo, the regions not covered by A, B, or C are people who don't own any of those, but own D. But the problem says everyone owns D, so the only people not owning A, B, or C are those who own only D.\n\nWait, but the problem doesn't mention people who own only D, so maybe that's another region.\n\nWait, let me think again. The total number of people is 900, and all own D. So, the only regions in the Venn diagram are:\n\n1. Only D: let's denote this as z.\n\n2. A only: |A| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. In a Venn diagram, the regions are:\n\n- Only A: |A| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|, but since D is everyone, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, this is getting too complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think of the number of people owning exactly one thing, exactly two, exactly three, and exactly four.\n\nBut since D is everyone, the only way to own exactly four things is to own A, B, C, and D. So, x = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, because D is everyone, so if you own A, B, C, you must own D as well. So, the number of people owning all four is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, but earlier we had that 0 = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - x, so x = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nHmm, so x is the number of people owning all four, which is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut since D is everyone, x is the number of people in A \u2229 B \u2229 C, which is the same as A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D.\n\nSo, okay, maybe that's consistent.\n\nSo, going back, we have:\n\nTotal people: 900.\n\nNumber of people owning exactly two things: 437.\n\nNumber of people owning exactly three things: 234.\n\nNumber of people owning exactly four things: x.\n\nNumber of people owning exactly one thing: let's denote this as m.\n\nThen, total number of people is m + 437 + 234 + x = 900.\n\nSo, m + 437 + 234 + x = 900.\n\nSo, m + x = 900 - 437 - 234 = 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nSo, m + x = 229.\n\nNow, m is the number of people owning exactly one thing.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the only way someone can own exactly one thing is to own only D, or own only one of A, B, C, and D.\n\nWait, but if someone owns only D, they don't own anything else, so that's exactly one thing.\n\nAlternatively, if someone owns only A, and nothing else, but since they own D, they own A and D, which is two things. So, wait, actually, if someone is in A, they must be in D as well, right?\n\nWait, no, D is a separate thing. So, actually, if someone owns A, they can also own other things. So, someone could own only A and D, which is two things, or only A, B, and D, which is three things, etc.\n\nWait, hold on, perhaps I'm overcomplicating. Maybe it's better to think in terms of the regions of the Venn diagram.\n\nSince D is everyone, the Venn diagram's universal set is the entire 900 residents. The regions are:\n\n1. Only A: |A| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nBut since D is everyone, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. The inclusion-exclusion formula for four sets is complicated, but since D is the entire set, the regions become:\n\n- Only A: |A| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nBut since |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, because all are subsets of D.\n\nWait, no, in reality, the number of people in all four sets is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|, which is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| because D is everyone.\n\nWait, perhaps not. Let me think carefully.\n\nIf I have four sets: A, B, C, D. D is the entire universe (900 people). So, the intersection A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D is just A \u2229 B \u2229 C, because D contains everyone.\n\nTherefore, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSimilarly, the intersection of three sets, say A \u2229 B \u2229 D, is equal to A \u2229 B.\n\nSimilarly, the intersection of two sets, A \u2229 B, is just A \u2229 B, since D is everyone.\n\nTherefore, the regions in the Venn diagram are:\n\n1. Only A: |A| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\n2. Only B: |B| - |A \u2229 B| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\n3. Only C: |C| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\n4. Only D: 900 - (|A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|)\n\nWait, no, that's not right. Since D is everyone, the only people not in A, B, or C are those who own only D.\n\nWait, actually, since everyone owns D, the only people not owning A, B, or C are those who own only D.\n\nSo, the number of people owning only D is 900 - (|A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2