substituting:\n\n671 + z = 195 + 367 + 562 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + z.\n\nWait, but we don't know |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C|.\n\nHmm, so we need another equation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can express |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C| in terms of the other variables.\n\nWait, |A \u2229 B| includes the people who own A, B, and possibly C or D.\n\nSimilarly, |A \u2229 B| = number of residents owning A and B, regardless of C or D.\n\nBut since D is owned by all, |A \u2229 B| includes those owning A, B, and D, and possibly C.\n\nSimilarly, |A \u2229 B| = number of residents owning A and B, which includes those owning exactly A and B, and those owning A, B, and C, and those owning all four.\n\nSo, |A \u2229 B| = number of residents owning exactly A and B + number owning A, B, and C + number owning all four.\n\nSimilarly, let's denote:\n\nLet\u2019s let:\n\n- p = number of residents owning exactly two of A, B, C and D: which is given as 437.\n\nBut wait, p is 437, but p includes all residents owning exactly two of A, B, C and D.\n\nSimilarly, q = number of residents owning exactly three of A, B, C and D: 234.\n\nAnd r = number of residents owning exactly four: z.\n\nSo, the number of residents owning exactly two of A, B, C and D is 437, which includes:\n\n- Exactly A and B,\n\n- Exactly A and C,\n\n- Exactly B and C.\n\nSimilarly, the number of residents owning exactly three of A, B, C and D is 234, which includes:\n\n- Exactly A, B, C,\n\n- Exactly A, B, D,\n\n- Exactly A, C, D,\n\n- Exactly B, C, D.\n\nWait, this is getting too complicated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can think about the number of people owning exactly two of A, B, C is equal to the sum of |A \u2229 B| - 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, but not sure.\n\nWait, maybe another approach.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\n- Let\u2019s let s = number of residents owning exactly two of A, B, C, which is 437.\n\n- Let\u2019s let t = number of residents owning ex