ut z has to be an integer because it's the number of residents.\n\nHmm, that suggests an error in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let me double-check my steps.\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion:\n\n900 - v = |A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWhich is,\n\n900 - v = 195 + 367 + 562 - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + z.\n\nWait, 195 + 367 is 562, plus 562 is 1124.\n\nSo,\n\n900 - v = 1124 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + z.\n\nWhich is,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 1124 - (900 - v) + z = 224 + v + z.\n\nSo, that part is correct.\n\nThen, the number of people owning exactly two is:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3z = 437.\n\nSo,\n\n(224 + v + z) - 3z = 437.\n\nWhich is,\n\n224 + v - 2z = 437.\n\nSo,\n\nv - 2z = 213.\n\nAnd from equation (1): v + z = 229.\n\nSo, substituting v = 213 + 2z into equation (1):\n\n213 + 2z + z = 229.\n\nSo,\n\n213 + 3z = 229.\n\nThus,\n\n3z = 16.\n\nWhich is not an integer. That can't be.\n\nHmm, so that suggests an error in the reasoning.\n\nWait, maybe I made a wrong assumption about the number of residents owning exactly two or three.\n\nWait, let me re-examine the problem.\n\nThe problem states:\n\n\"437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things.\"\n\nSo, exactly two: 437, exactly three: 234.\n\nIn addition, each resident owns a bag of candy hearts, which is D.\n\nSo, the breakdown is:\n\n- Only D: a\n\n- Exactly two of A, B, C: 437\n\n- Exactly three of A, B, C: 234\n\n- All four: z\n\nTotal: a + 437 + 234 + z = 900.\n\nSo, a + 671 + z = 900.\n\nThus, a + z = 229. So, equation (1): a + z = 229.\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C| = 900 - a.\n\nBut |A \u222a B \u222a C| = 900 - a.\n\nBut |A \u222a B \u222a C| = 900 - a.\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A|A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C|