ation (1): a + z = 229.\n\nNow, |A \u222a B \u222a C| is equal to the number of residents owning at least one of A, B, or C, which is 900 - a.\n\nBut also, |A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWhich is 195 + 367 + 562 - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + z.\n\nSo,\n\n900 - a = 1124 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + z.\n\nBut from the exact counts, the number of residents owning exactly two is 437, which is equal to:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - 3z).\n\nBecause each resident owning exactly two is counted in two pairwise intersections, so we subtract 3z to get rid of those who are in all three.\n\nSo,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3z = 437.\n\nThus,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 437 + 3z.\n\nBut from the inclusion-exclusion:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 1124 - (900 - a) + z = 224 + a + z.\n\nWait, let me do that again.\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut |A \u222a B \u222a C| = 900 - a.\n\nThus,\n\n900 - a = 195 + 367 + 562 - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + z.\n\nSo,\n\n900 - a = 1124 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + z.\n\nThus,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 1124 - (900 - a) + z = 224 + a + z.\n\nBut from the exact counts, we also have:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 437 + 3z.\n\nSo,\n\n224 + a + z = 437 + 3z.\n\nThus,\n\na + z = 437 - 224 + 3z.\n\nWait, no:\n\n224 + a + z = 437 + 3z.\n\nThus,\n\na + z = 437 + 3z - 224.\n\nSo,\n\na + z = 213 + 3z.\n\nThus,\n\na = 213 + 2z.\n\nBut from equation (1), a + z = 229.\n\nThus,\n\n213 + 2z + z = 229.\n\nSo,\n\n213 + 3z = 229.\n\nThus,\n\n3z = 16.\n\nz = 16/3.\n\nThus, z = (229 - 213)/3 = 16/3.\n\nWait, same result as before. 16/3 is approximately 5.333.\n\nHmm, same problem.\n\nThus, it's impossible because z is not integer.\n\nHmm, so that suggests that either the problem has no solution or I made a mistake.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the initial counts.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let's write down all equations clearly.\n\nEquation (1): a + z = 229.\n\nFrom inclusion-exclusion and exact counts, we have:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 224 + a + z.\n\nBut also, (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 437 + 3z.\n\nTherefore,\n\n224 + a + z = 437 + 3z.\n\nSo,\n\na + z = 437 - 224 + 3z - z.\n\nWait, 224 + a + z = 437 + 3z.\n\nThus,\n\na + z = 437 + 3z - 224.\n\nSo,\n\na + z = 213 + 3z.\n\nThus,\n\na = 213 + 2z.\n\nBut from equation (1), a + z = 229.\n\nSo,\n\n213 + 2z + z = 229.\n\nThus,\n\n213 + 3z = 229.\n\nTherefore,\n\n3z = 16.\n\nThus,\n\nz = 16/3 \u2248 5.333.\n\nHmm, z is not an integer, which is impossible because the number of residents must be integer.\n\nThis suggests that there is an inconsistency in the problem.\n\nBut the problem is given, so perhaps I made a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, maybe I miscalculated somewhere.\n\nLet me check again.\n\nTotal residents: 900.\n\nNumber who own exactly two: 437.\n\nNumber who own exactly three: 234.\n\nNumber who own all four: z.\n\nNumber who own only D: a.\n\nTotal: a + 437 + 234 + z = 900.\n\nThus,\n\na + 437 + 234 + z = 900.\n\nSo,\n\na + z = 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nEquation (1): a + z = 229.\n\nInclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut |A \u222a B \u222a C| is also equal to 900 - a.\n\nThus,\n\n900 - a = 195 + 367 + 562 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo,\n\n900 - a = 1124 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nThus,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 1124 - (900 - a) + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut from the exact counts, the number of people owning exactly two is 437, which is equal to:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBecause each person owning all three is counted in all three pairwise intersections, so we subtract 3 times the triple overlap.\n\nThus,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 437 + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut from inclusion-exclusion, we have:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 1124 - 900 + a + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo,\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 224 + a + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nThus,\n\n224 + a + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 437 + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nTherefore,\n\n224 + a = 437 + 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nLet me denote |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = t.\n\nThus,\n\n224 + a = 437 + 2t.\n\nSo,\n\na = 437 + 2t - 224 = 213 + 2t.\n\nFrom equation (1):\n\na + z = 229.\n\nThus,\n\n213 + 2t + z = 229.\n\nSo,\n\nz = 229 - 213 - 2t = 16 - 2t.\n\nBut z must be non-negative, so 16 - 2t \u2265 0.\n\nThus,\n\nt \u2264 8.\n\nAlso, t must be integer.\n\nBut we don't know t yet.\n\nIs there another equation?\n\nYes, we can consider the number of residents owning exactly three things, which is 234.\n\nThe number of residents owning exactly three is equal to:\n\n(|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) - (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| \u2229 only D).\n\nWait, no. Wait, the number of residents owning exactly three is the number of people who own A, B, C, and not D? But everyone owns D.\n\nWait, no, actually, all residents own D, so the number of residents owning exactly three is the number of people owning A, B, C, and nothing else besides D.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the number of residents owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no. Because if someone owns all three, A, B, C, and D, they are counted in the exactly three category? Or is it that exactly three means they own exactly A, B, C, and not D?\n\nWait, the problem says \"own exactly three of these things.\" Since D is also a thing, so if someone owns A, B, C, and D, they own four things. But the problem says \"exactly three,\" so that person must own exactly three, which would mean owning three things and not the fourth. But since D is a separate thing, does that mean that they don't own D? But the problem states that each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts, which is D. So, D is a must.\n\nTherefore, the number of residents owning exactly three of A, B, C, and D is the number of residents owning exactly three, which is 234.\n\nThus, the number of residents owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nThus, the number of residents owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 0.\n\nWait, that can't be.\n\nWait, no. If everyone owns D, then the only way to own exactly three is to own exactly A, B, C, and D. But since D is a must, the number of residents owning exactly three is zero.\n\nBut the problem says there are 234 residents who own exactly three. So, that suggests that the number of residents owning exactly three is 234, but since everyone owns D, the only way is that they don't own any other thing besides D, but that contradicts.\n\nWait, maybe I misinterpreted the problem.\n\nWait, let me reread the problem.\n\n\"Among the 900 residents of Aimeville, there are 195 who own a diamond ring, 367 who own a set of golf clubs, and 562 who own a garden spade. In addition, each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts. There are 437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things. Find the number of residents of Aimeville who own all four of these things.\"\n\nWait, \"four of these things.\" So, perhaps, the four things are diamond ring, golf clubs, garden spade, and candy hearts.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"own exactly two of thes