pproach is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the equations.\n\nWait, let me re-examine.\n\nWe have:\n\nThe number of people owning exactly two is |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 437.\n\nAnd the number of people owning at least two is |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 900 - x, where x is the number of people owning only D.\n\nBut wait, earlier, I said |A \u222a B \u222a C| = 900 - x.\n\nBut in the problem, we have:\n\nTotal residents: 900.\n\nNumber owning exactly two: 437.\n\nNumber owning exactly three: 234.\n\nNumber owning exactly four: d.\n\nNumber owning only D: x.\n\nSo,\n\nx + 437 + 234 + d = 900\n\nWhich gives x + d = 900 - 437 - 234 = 229\n\nSo, x = 229 - d.\n\nBut we also have that |A \u222a B \u222a C| = 900 - x.\n\nWhich is equal to |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo,\n\n|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 900 - x.\n\nBut from the exact counts:\n\nThe number of people owning exactly two is |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 437.\n\nAnd the number of people owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no.\n\nWait, no. The number of people owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|? That doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, actually, the number of people owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, because they own A, B, C, and D.\n\nBut if someone owns exactly three, they don't own any more. But since D is given to everyone, if they own A, B, C, they must own four things.\n\nWait, wait, no. The problem says \"own a diamond ring, a set of golf clubs, and a garden spade\". So, each of these is a separate thing.\n\nSo, someone can own multiple of these. So, if someone owns all four, they own all four.\n\nBut the problem says \"own all four of these things\".\n\nWait, the exact wording is:\n\n\"the number of residents who own a